# Domain Rules — AI Voice Inventory System

## Overview
This document defines the domain rules for the AI-voice-driven warehouse inventory
system. All code changes must conform to these rules.

## 1. Worker Speaks (Voice Capture)
- The worker initiates voice capture via a microphone button in the web UI.
- Audio is captured as 16-bit mono PCM at 16 kHz.
- Microphone access must be checked and tested before recording.
- A floating draggable voice-assistant button provides quick access to voice entry.

## 2. Speech-to-Text
- Audio is sent to a local faster-whisper service for transcription.
- The transcript, language, confidence, and duration are stored as VoiceEvidence.
- VoiceEvidence is linked to the worker (createdById) and optionally to a transaction.

## 3. AI Extraction
- The AI service uses Qwen3 (via Ollama) to extract structured fields from the transcript.
- Extracted fields: action, productSku, quantity, sourceLocationCode,
  destinationLocationCode, condition, referenceNumber, notes, fieldConfidence.
- Products and locations must be matched against active database records — never invented.
- Action-specific location rules:
  - RECEIVE → destinationLocation only
  - SHIP, USE, CYCLE_COUNT, DAMAGE, LOSS → sourceLocation only
  - TRANSFER → both sourceLocation and destinationLocation
- Reference numbers and notes are only accepted if they appear verbatim in the transcript.
- Clarification questions are generated for missing or low-confidence fields.

## 4. Confirmation / Read-Back
- The worker can hear the full proposal via text-to-speech before confirming.
- The worker can correct details and re-extract.
- The worker must explicitly confirm before a pending transaction is created.

## 5. Pending Record (Staging)
- A pending transaction is created with status PENDING.
- The transaction stores: worker ID (createdById), timestamp (createdAt),
  original transcript, extracted fields, and AI confidence.
- The transaction stores the device identifier if available.
- Idempotency is supported via clientRequestId.

## 6. Physical Verification
- When a manager requests a recount, a RECOUNT task is created for the worker.
- The worker speaks the actual physical count, which goes through the same
  voice → STT → AI extraction pipeline.
- The recount task is linked to the original transaction via sourceTransactionId.

## 7. Business Validation
- Permissions: Keycloak JWT with role-based access control (worker, manager, administrator).
- Stock availability: shipping or using more than available stock is rejected.
- Unit of measure: each product has a unit (e.g., "unit", "kg", "litre").
- References: referenceNumber is stored on transactions.
- Location validation: source/destination locations must be active.
- Product validation: products must be active.
- Action-specific rules:
  - RECEIVE requires a destination location.
  - SHIP, USE, CYCLE_COUNT, DAMAGE, LOSS require a source location.
  - TRANSFER requires both source and destination locations.
  - Source and destination must be different.

## 8. Risk & Approval Routing
The following transactions are routed to manager review (status PENDING, awaiting approval):

### 8.1 Action-based routing (existing)
- CYCLE_COUNT, DAMAGE, LOSS → always require manager review.

### 8.2 Quantity-based routing (new)
- A transaction is flagged for review if the quantity deviates significantly
  from the item's recent average quantity.
- The deviation threshold is configurable (default: 50% deviation from recent average).
- "Recent" is defined as the last N transactions (default: 10) within a time window
  (default: 30 days).

### 8.3 Repeated correction routing (new)
- A transaction is flagged for review if the same item has been corrected
  (i.e., a previous transaction for the same item was cancelled or recounted)
  more than N times within a short time window.
- The correction threshold is configurable (default: 3 corrections).
- The time window is configurable (default: 1 hour).

### 8.4 Controlled / high-value item routing (new)
- A product flagged as `controlled = true` always requires manager review
  for any stock-reducing action (SHIP, USE, DAMAGE, LOSS, CYCLE_COUNT).
- The `controlled` flag is a boolean field on the Product model.

### 8.5 Near-zero stock routing (new)
- A transaction is flagged for review if it would bring the available stock
  for an item at a location to within a "near-zero" threshold of zero.
- The near-zero threshold is configurable (default: 5 units).
- This applies to stock-reducing actions (SHIP, USE, DAMAGE, LOSS, CYCLE_COUNT).

### 8.6 Negative stock prevention
- A transaction that would result in negative available stock is rejected
  (ConflictException), not routed for review.

## 9. Post Update
- Low-risk transactions (RECEIVE, SHIP, USE, TRANSFER) are posted atomically
  after worker confirmation.
- High-risk transactions (CYCLE_COUNT, DAMAGE, LOSS, and any flagged by
  the rules in section 8) remain pending for manager approval.
- After posting, reorder drafts are synced for the affected product and locations.
- Concurrency is handled with Serializable isolation level.

## 10. Low-Stock Check
- After every posted transaction, the system checks if available stock
  is below the safety stock level.
- If low, a reorder draft is created or updated with a suggested quantity.
- The suggested quantity is: max(reorderQuantity, safetyStock - available).
- The reorder quantity must be at least the supplier's minimum order quantity.

## 11. Reorder Draft / Purchase-Order Generation
- Reorder drafts are automatically created when stock falls below safety level.
- Managers can approve or cancel drafts.
- Approved drafts create an expected-receiving task in the worker queue.
- Supplier emails are queued via BullMQ/Valkey and sent via Nodemailer.
- Email delivery status is tracked (QUEUED, SENT, FAILED).

## 12. Dashboard & Audit History
- Worker dashboard: overview, voice entry, task queue, active items, history.
- Manager dashboard: overview, approvals, reorders, audit history, task planning.
- Administrator dashboard: system health, user management, product/location/supplier management.
- All transactions are stored with full audit trail (created by, approved by, timestamps).
- CSV export is available for audit history.
